# Stage 1 - baseline

`ppl stage enter baseline` first - the FSM has no admission -> briefing edge, only
admission -> baseline -> briefing, so skipping this call dead-ends the move to briefing
with `ILLEGAL_TRANSITION` [R-FSM-STAGE-GRAPH]. Capture the
generation it prints, same as any other `stage enter`.

The worktree and its branch `<TASK-ID>-<slug>` already exist: `ppl lane` made them from
`<!-- include: harness/default-branch -->` before this session started, at the path `ppl show --json` reports as `repo`
(`<worktree>` here and in every later stage). Do not make another one. Fetch the default
branch, so the gates that compare against it see its current head:

```bash
<!-- include: os/cd --><!-- include: os/fetch -->
```

<!-- include: os/gates-where --><!-- include: os/stage1-note -->

Then record the baseline. <!-- include: harness/baseline -->
Commands are in `references/gates.md`. The base SHA is recorded first and the baseline suite
runs beside the briefer, in this order:

1. Save `<!-- include: harness/scratch -->/<TASK-ID>/baseline.json` now, with `base_sha` set to the worktree's HEAD before any of
   your own commits and a note that the baseline suite's logs and exit codes are the gate
   `.log` and `.rc` files under `<!-- include: harness/scratch -->/<TASK-ID>`. Run
   `ppl baseline record <!-- include: harness/scratch -->/<TASK-ID>/baseline.json`. `references/baseline.md` says exactly which field of that document is checked
   (only `base_sha`) and which are a durable note by convention only - read it before assuming
   the rest of the document is validated the way a brief or a verdict is.
2. Run `ppl stage enter briefing` and dispatch `ppl-briefer` once, as
   `references/stage-2-briefing.md` says: read that reference now.
3. Then, while the briefer works, run the baseline suite: every gate command that covers the
   list above, each as its own Bash call in the shape `references/gates.md` gives it, as before.
   Make no background Bash call: the briefer already works beside you. If the dispatch
   returns only when the briefer is done, run the same commands as soon as it returns, before
   anything else in Stage 2. If the briefer finishes first, the remaining commands still run:
   Stage 2 does not replace them. Stage 3 starts only after the last one has written its
   `.rc` file [R-BASELINE-SUITE-BEFORE-IMPLEMENTATION n/a].

Without a baseline, a pre-existing red test and a regression are indistinguishable.

`ppl baseline record` succeeds exactly once per run, and only for a commit HEAD descends
from, and only while you are still in the baseline stage. It refuses a second call with
`ILLEGAL_TRANSITION`, a non-ancestor `base_sha` with `SCHEMA_INVALID`, and a call made after
you have already moved on to `briefing` or later with `ILLEGAL_TRANSITION` too: the
done-gate proves your diff stays inside the write boundary by diffing `base_sha..HEAD`, so a
baseline recorded after the work commit would empty that diff and pass the check with
nothing in it [R-BASELINE-WRITE-ONCE] [R-BASELINE-ANCESTOR] [R-BASELINE-NOT-AT-HEAD]. On a
freshly cut branch with no commits of your own yet, `base_sha` equal to the worktree's
current HEAD is correct and expected - that is exactly what "before any of your own
commits" means when there are none yet. If you believe the baseline is genuinely wrong,
that is an owner decision - escalate, do not re-record.
